MEASURED and documented. Closed.

Docker 29.7.2 (API 1.55) vs rootless Podman 5.4.2 (API 1.41), 2026-08-27,
kill?signal=SIGUSR1 against a paused container:

                        Docker              Podman
  status                204                 204
  state right after     running             paused
  Paused flag           false               true
  handler ran?          yes, at once        no -- queued until unpause

So both answer 204, but Docker UNPAUSES the container as a side effect the
caller never asked for, and the process handles the signal normally. Podman
leaves it paused and queues the signal.

The ticket's own reasoning was half right: "a signal delivered to a paused
process is queued rather than handled" holds on Podman and is refuted on
Docker. The 204 hides a different surprise there -- an undocumented
paused -> running transition. Measured consequence: a caller's following
unpause then croaks 500 "Container <id> is not paused".

Documented in the kill POD of API::Docker::API::Containers as the one place
the two engines genuinely part, alongside both engines' 409 texts. No code
change: the behaviour is the engine's, and neither answer is wrong.

